WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.300
Today we're demystifying vector databases, the storage layer behind semantic search and RAG systems.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:13.500
A vector database stores high-dimensional float arrays called embeddings, and finds similar vectors fast.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:20.400
Why not just use Postgres? Postgres with pgvector works surprisingly well up to a few million vectors.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:27.900
But at tens of millions of vectors with strict latency budgets, specialized indexes like HNSW win.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:34.800
HNSW is a hierarchical graph. Build time is slower, but queries hop through the graph in logarithmic time.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:41.600
The trade-off is memory. HNSW graphs can consume several times the raw size of your vectors.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:48.200
IVF is the other big family. It clusters vectors into buckets and searches only the nearest buckets.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:55.100
Tune the probe parameter to balance recall against speed. More probes means higher recall, more latency.

00:00:55.100 --> 00:01:02.400
Distance metrics matter. Cosine similarity ignores magnitude, dot product rewards larger norms, L2 is euclidean.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:09.700
For most text search, cosine similarity or normalized dot product is the right default choice.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:16.900
Metadata filtering is essential for production. You'll want to filter by tenant, date, or document type.

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:24.100
Pre-filtering narrows candidates before the ANN search, while post-filtering risks empty result sets.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:31.300
Popular options: Pinecone is fully managed, Qdrant is fast and self-hostable, Weaviate has hybrid search built in.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:38.500
Also consider Milvus for sheer scale, and pgvector when you want one database for everything.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:45.700
Benchmark against your own data. Consensus is that vendor marketing numbers rarely match reality.
